    "title": "Reflection:",
    "placeholder": "Enter your reflection here.",
    "long_instructions": "# Revisiting the Final Project\r\n\r\n## Interactive Data Dashboard\r\n\r\n#### In Unit 4’s final project, students take on the role of data designers and developers to build an **Interactive Data Dashboard** that responds to user input.\r\n\r\n#### Throughout the unit, students practiced the same techniques they will apply in this project: filtering datasets, summarizing information, making design decisions about labels and defaults, and using AI tools thoughtfully to support — not replace — their own logic.\r\n\r\n#### By the time they reach the project, students are prepared to connect code, data, and design by creating dashboards that update dynamically as filters change, similar to real-world tools like music apps, shopping sites, and analytics dashboards.\r\n\r\n<img src=\"https://images.code.org/e5e75f6dba393136310fc8866cf35c52-image-1768412595902.png\" alt=\"Sample student project of an interactive data dashboard showing songs filtered by artist, genre, and mood\" width=\"650px\" style=\"display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;border-radius:10px; \">\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## How the unit builds toward this project\r\n\r\n#### Across the lessons, students progressively develop the skills they need to succeed:\r\n\r\n- #### Write logic to **filter and summarize data**\r\n- #### Use **loops and array methods** to compute counts and averages\r\n- #### Design **clear labels, categories, and fallback values**\r\n- #### Make filtering **transparent** so users understand what they’re seeing\r\n- #### Consider who or what might be **surfaced or hidden** by design choices\r\n- #### Use AI as a **scaffold** while documenting decisions and refinements\r\n\r\n#### Each concept appears first in isolation, then comes together in the final dashboard.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## You Did It!\r\n\r\n#### You’ve now explored the tools, concepts, and workflows students will use to complete the Interactive Data Dashboard project.\r\n\r\n#### This module is designed to help you:\r\n- #### See how the unit builds intentionally toward a complex final product\r\n- #### Anticipate where students may struggle or need support\r\n- #### Feel confident guiding students as they iterate on both **code** and **design decisions**\r\n\r\n<p style=\"font-size: 18px; background-color: #F5F7F8; padding: 10px; border-left: 5px solid #8148B2; display: inline-block;\"> ➡️ <strong>Optional: </strong>Open the <a href='https://studio.code.org/courses/artificial-intelligence-foundations-semester2-2025/units/4/lessons/11' target='_blank' rel='noopener noreferrer' style='text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;'>Project Lesson Plan</a> to review when you're done with this module. </p>\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Reflection\r\n\r\n>#### Name one concept from earlier in the unit that students will draw on during the final project, and explain how you expect them to use it in their dashboard.\r\n\r\n<div style=\"display: flex; align-items: left; margin:20px 0px 30px 0px\">\r\n\r\n<a href=\"https://forum.code.org\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">\r\n<img src=\"https://images.code.org/7dbe63a051fbad4ef262d6ab710e2966-image-1731959037460.png\" style=\"width: auto; height: 100px;\" alt=\"A browser with two text boxes, both including an avatar and lines of text.\"></a>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background-color: #007786; border-radius: 20px 20px 20px 20px; color: #000000; width: 80%; margin-left: 5%; margin-right: 5%; padding: 10px 10px 5px 10px;\">\r\n\r\n<p style=\"color: white; line-height: 1.5; margin-left: 10px; margin-right: 10px; font-size: 18px\"><b>Post to the Code.org Forum</b></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"color: white; line-height: 1.2; margin-left: 10px; margin-right: 10px; font-size: 18px\">\r\nShare your response and read other educators' ideas on the forum! Click on the icon to the left to direct to our forum post.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n</div>\r\n</div>\r\n",
